ACC-013 — Engineering Context

Document ID: ACC-013
Title: Engineering Context
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Context, the set of conditions, relationships, assumptions, and constraints within which engineering cognition is interpreted.

Engineering Context ensures that Engineering Observations, Engineering Evidence, Engineering Understanding, Engineering Reasoning, Engineering Capabilities, and Decision Support are interpreted according to their applicable engineering environment.

2. Scope

This specification applies to every cognitive artifact and process within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Engineering Context may include, but is not limited to:

Project information
Engineering discipline
Geographic location
Regulatory environment
Applicable standards and codes
Design assumptions
Operational conditions
Environmental conditions
Temporal conditions
Stakeholders
System boundaries
Resource availability
Lifecycle phase

Engineering Context applies across the entire engineering cognition lifecycle.

3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Context is the complete set of conditions, relationships, assumptions, constraints, and environmental factors required to correctly interpret engineering information and cognition.

Engineering information cannot be fully understood without its context.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Context SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Explicit

Engineering Context SHALL be explicitly represented whenever practical.

4.2 Relevant

Only context relevant to the engineering objective SHOULD influence engineering cognition.

4.3 Dynamic

Engineering Context MAY change throughout the engineering lifecycle.

4.4 Multi-Dimensional

Engineering Context MAY include technical, operational, regulatory, environmental, organizational, temporal, and spatial dimensions.

4.5 Traceable

Engineering Context SHALL maintain traceability to the engineering artifacts and reasoning processes it influences.

4.6 Shared

Engineering Context MAY be shared across multiple Engineering Understandings, Reasoning processes, and Capabilities.

4.7 Evolvable

Engineering Context SHALL evolve as engineering conditions change.

